ly. A vertex totally dominates another vertex if they are neighbors.\nA total dominating set of a graph G is a set S of vertices such that every vertex of\nG is totally dominated by a vertex in S. Analogously as in the domination game,\nthe total domination game consists of two players called Dominator and Staller,\nwho take turns choosing a vertex from G. However, in the total version each vertex\n140 M. A. Henning\n\nchosen must totally dominate at least one vertex not totally dominated by the set of\nvertices previously chosen. Such a chosen vertex is called a legal move or a playable\nvertex in the total domination game. The game ends when there is no legal move\navailable. In this case, the set of vertices chosen is a total dominating set in G.\nDominator’s objective is to minimize the number of vertices chosen, while the goal\nof Staller is just the opposite, namely to end the game with as many vertices chosen\nas possible.\nThe Dominator-start (resp., Staller-start) total domination game is the total\ndomination game when Dominator (resp., Staller) has the first move. As with the\ndomination game, we refer to these simply as the D-game and S-game, respectively.\nThe game total domination number, γtg (G), of G is the number of moves played in\nthe D-game when both players play optimally. The corresponding invariant for the\nS-game is denoted γtg\u0013 (G).\nAdopting the terminology from [20], a partially total dominated graph is a graph\ntogether with a declaration that some vertices are already totally dominated; that is,\nthey need not be totally dominated in the rest of the game. Given a graph G and\na subset S of vertices of G, we denote by G|S the partially total dominated graph\nin which the vertices of S in G are already totally dominated. We use γtg (G|S) to\ndenote the number of turns remaining in the D-game on G|S. In [18], the authors\npresent a key lemma, named the Total Continuation Principle, which in particular\nimplies that |γtg (G) − γtg\u0013 (G)| ≤ 1 for every graph G with no isolated vertex.\nIn [19] the following 34 -Game Total Domination Conjecture is posed.\nConjecture 4 ([19]) If G is a graph of order n in which every component contains\nat least three vertices, then γtg (G) ≤ 34 n.\nAs remarked in [19], the requirement in Conjecture 4 that every component\ncontains at least three vertices is essential. For example, if G is the disjoint union\nof copies of K2 , then γtg (G) = n. The first general upper bound on the game total\ndomination number was given in [19].\nTheorem 8 ([19]) If G is a graph of order n in which every component contains at\nleast three vertices, then γtg (G) ≤ 45 n.\nRecently, Csilla Bujtás obtained a new general upper bound on the game total\ndomination number that improves this earlier bound of 4n/5.\nTheorem 9 ([8]) If G is a graph of order n in which every component contains at\nleast three vertices, then γtg (G) ≤ 11\n14 n.\nBujtás’s bound in Theorem 9 is the best general upper bound on the game total\ndomination number to date.\nAs remarked in [19], if the 34 -Game Total Domination Conjecture is true, then\nthe upper bound is tight as may be seen by taking, for example, G ∼ = k1 P4 ∪ k2 P8\nwhere k1 , k2 ≥ 0 and k1 + k2 ≥ 1. Since γtg (P4 ) = γtg\u0013 (P4 ) = 3 and γtg (P8 ) =\nγtg\u0013 (P8 ) = 6, the optimal strategy of Staller is whenever Dominator starts playing\non a component of G, she plays on that component and adopts her optimal strategy\nMy Favorite Domination Game Conjectures 141\n\non the component. This shows that γtg (G) = 3k1 + 6k2 = 3n/4, where n = 4k1 +\n8k2 is the number of vertices in G.\nWe discuss next special cases where the 34 -Game Total Domination Conjecture\nhas been proven. Together with my distinguished Hungarian coauthors, Csilla Bujtás\nand Zsolt Tuza, we proved in [9] that the conjecture holds over the class of graphs\nwith minimum degree at least 2. To do this, we raise the problem to a higher level\nby introducing a transversal game in hypergraphs, and establish a tight upper bound\non the game transversal number of a hypergraph with all edges of size at least 2 in\nterms of its order and size. As an application of this result, we prove that if G is a\ngraph of order n with minimum degree at least 2, then γtg (G) ≤ 11 8\nn.\nTheorem 10 ([9]) If G is a graph of order n with δ(G) ≥ 2, then γtg (G) ≤ 8\n11 n.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\norientation — arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/keyword/orientation?page=9)\nciteturn7search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Characterization of graphs with orientable total domination number equal to $|V|-1$\ngraph orientation random random regular graph regular graph tournament\n\n2024-11-07\n\nZoltán L. Blázsik, Leila Vivien Nagy\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nCharacterization of graphs with orientable total domination number equal to $|V|-1$\n\nIn a directed graph $D$, a vertex subset $S\\subseteq V$ is a total dominating set if every vertex of $D$ has an in-neighbor from $S$. A total dominating set exists if and only if every vertex has at least one in-neighbor. We call the orientation of such directed graphs valid. The total domination number of $D$, denoted by $γ_t(D)$, is the size of the smallest total dominating set of $D$. For an undirected graph $G$, we investigate the upper (or lower) orientable total domination number of $G$, denoted by $\\mathrm{DOM}_t(G)$ (or $\\mathrm{dom}_t(G)$), that is the maximum (or minimum) of the total domination numbers over all valid orientations of $G$. We characterize those graphs for which $\\mathrm{DOM}_t(G)=|V(G)|-1$, and consequently we show that there exists a family of graphs for which $\\mathrm{DOM}_t(G)$ and $\\mathrm{dom}_t(G)$ can be as far as possible, namely $\\mathrm{DOM}_t(G)=|V(G)|-1$ and $\\mathrm{dom}_t(G)=3$.\n\ngraph orientable orientation----------------------------------------------------------------------------